From f15727db0bcee2b79236d78100490c2d6475253a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: faliye <faliye@example.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 00:43:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH v2] thunderbolt: Stop waiting on a path pending bit that never
 clears

__tb_path_deactivate_hop() disables a hop and then waits up to 500 ms for
its pending bit to read back as clear. On an ASMedia ASM4242 host router
the bit belonging to the host interface adapter latches once enough
frames have gone through the DMA ring feeding it, and from then on it
never clears. Every teardown of a host-to-host DMA tunnel spends the
full 500 ms on that hop and gives up anyway, which on a link that is
brought up and down repeatedly is 500 ms every time for an answer that
is already known.

There are two regimes, and the number of frames sent decides which one
the adapter is in. Below the threshold the bit behaves: a path that has
just been activated and has never carried a frame reads it set, and is
back to clear a few seconds later with no traffic at all. Above it the
bit stops moving. On a hop entry left behind by an earlier teardown it
still reads set while the rest of the entry reads as zero - 129 times
over a day of testing, against zero occurrences on any other adapter of
the same router, which has four lane adapters, two PCIe down, two USB3
down and two DP in.

Reading the hop config space either side of the disable write shows the
write lands and the controller does react to it, and that pending is all
that is left:

  dw0  80000808 -> 00000808   enable cleared
  dw1  127ff501 -> 107ff501   egress FC cleared by the controller
        ^ bit 28, the only bit still set

Nothing reachable from the driver clears it from there. It still reads
set after 30 s of polling (237528 config space reads), after 60 s of idle
before the teardown is even started, and across a module unload and
reload. The other hop of the same tunnel, the one on the lane adapter,
drains on its first read in about 125 us.

What decides it is the ring, not an amount of traffic. With the tbnet
ring at 128 and at 256 descriptors the first frame count whose teardown
fails moves with it:

  ring 128    100 frames  0/5 fail    120 frames  5/5 fail
  ring 256    240 frames  0/5 fail    260 frames  5/5 fail

Every point is either five failures out of five or none, and the pending
bit read back before the disable write agrees with the outcome in each of
those 40 rounds. Traffic keeps flowing at full rate afterwards, so
nothing is stuck behind the bit either.

I would like to say the trigger is the ring wrapping round, and for the
256 ring the threshold does straddle 256. It does not for the 128 one:
120 frames is already enough there, short of a full lap. So the two sizes
show that the point moves with the ring, not that the wrap itself is what
does it. A third size would say more than I can.

The wait itself is right. USB4 v2 spec table 8-23, the path entry of a
protocol adapter - which is what a host interface adapter is - defines
the bit as

  28  Pending Packets (PP)                                   Type: RO
      An Adapter shall set this field to 1b when one or more packets
      that belong to the Path are waiting to be dequeued.
      Otherwise it shall be set to zero.

word for word what table 8-22 gives for a lane adapter. So it is neither
vendor defined - the vendor defined path fields of that same table are
Path Credits Allocated, IFC and ISE, which the connection manager already
leaves alone - nor writable, being read only.
An adapter holding it at 1b with nothing queued is in breach of that
"shall", and there is nothing the connection manager can do about it
from here: the poll has no ending other than the timeout.

The connection manager already gives up when the wait times out. So the
question is not whether to wait it out, nor what to tell the caller, but
whether to spend the timeout re-deriving an answer that is already known.
Remember it on the adapter and return the same -ETIMEDOUT straight away
next time.

Nothing above this function sees any difference. On an adapter with this
flag set the caller gets exactly what it gets today - the same error, on
the same teardowns, with the same warning from
__tb_path_deactivate_hops() - only without the half second in front of
it. That is deliberate: the timeout is a true statement about this
adapter, and suppressing it would be reintroducing lower down the thing
that reporting it was meant to fix.

Skipping it is not free, and the spec says where the cost lands. Section
5.4.1 lets a router keep transmitting on a path while its pending bit
reads 1b, and only requires it to stop once tTeardown - 2 us - has
elapsed since the bit went to 0b, and to stay stopped until Valid is set
again. On an adapter where the bit never reaches 0b, that condition is
never met, so re-activating the path without having seen it clear is
outside what the spec describes: a packet left over from the old path
could still be transmitted onto the new one. The 200 cycles below each
carried traffic and all of them completed, but a run completing is not
the same as showing that no stale packet ever crossed, and I cannot
show the latter.

Every timeout is still reported, this one included: the function returns
-ETIMEDOUT whether it waited for it or not, and __tb_path_deactivate_hops()
warns on it exactly as before. The other caller of
__tb_path_deactivate_hop() is tb_path_activate(), which discards the
return value, so a first timeout arriving there would set the flag
without a word. In the runs below it never did - the hop reads back
disabled by then and the function returns early - but that is an
observation, not something the code guarantees.

This needs no vendor matching: an adapter whose pending bit behaves never
sets the flag, so nothing changes for hardware that works, whoever made
it. It is however limited to host interface adapters. A lane, PCIe, USB3
or DP adapter that latched the bit the same way would still burn the
timeout on every teardown. I scoped it that way because that is where I
have evidence - 466 timeouts on the host interface adapter of this router
against 3 on its lane adapter over the same day, and the lane adapter
drained on the first read every other time - not because I know the
others cannot do it.

Everything above and below was measured on v6.17 with three later commits
on top, without which the link does not stay up long enough to measure
anything across it. All three are in mainline now, so on a current tree
the base is plain mainline:

  commit 7e49bb89df86 ("thunderbolt: Avoid reserved fields in path config space for USB4 routers")
  commit 1881f2efbf7f ("Revert "net: thunderbolt: Enable end-to-end flow control also in transmit"")
  commit 68bf02b6b4ad ("net: thunderbolt: Tear down DMA paths before stopping the rings")

>>> NUMBERS BELOW ARE FROM THE RETURN-0 VERSION AND DO NOT DESCRIBE THIS
>>> PATCH.  DO NOT SEND UNTIL THE RE-RUN REPLACES THEM.  The wall clock
>>> row should survive unchanged; the failure count should now match the
>>> control arm, not drop to 1/1.

Measured by cycling the interface down and up 200 times over 80 minutes
between two of these hosts, from a cold boot, with no module reloads in
between. Only the thunderbolt module differs between the two runs:

                                        without      with
    hop deactivation failed              213 / 206    1 / 1
    wall clock per cycle                 23.4 s       22.9 s
                                     (host A / host B)

The run comes out 110 seconds shorter over its 200 cycles - 4682 against
4572 seconds of wall clock, which is where the per-cycle figures above
come from. The script's own delays are fixed, so that difference is the
timeout itself: 200 cycles at half a second each would be 100 seconds,
and the measured 110 is that plus whatever the rest of the run varied by.

