This came out of an investigation on a pair of ASMedia ASM4242 hosts
wired to each other. It stands on its own: it applies to mainline
unchanged and needs nothing else under it.

Two thunderbolt-net fixes from the same investigation went to netdev
separately:
https://lore.kernel.org/netdev/20260809-b4-tbnet-hopid-v1-0-9a8c7f5f0ba9@gmail.com/

Neither is needed for this patch to apply or to work. They matter only
for reproducing the measurements, because without them a prolonged run of
link cycling produces a warning splat of its own that gets in the way.

Measured on two ASM4242 hosts linked to each other, hw_vendor_id 0x174c,
hw_device_id 0x2428, NVM 200011.250708, on the base named in the commit
message plus the two thunderbolt-net patches linked above.

This started out as a quirk keyed on that hardware id, and the one-shot
form is better on every count I can think of. Three things about it are
worth saying plainly.

One timeout is enough to set the flag, and that is a deliberate choice
rather than an oversight. A host interface adapter whose bit works but
whose poll happened to time out once - a busy control channel, say -
would stop being waited on for the rest of that switch's lifetime. I have
not seen that happen on a host interface adapter, but I have seen a
single timeout on an adapter that was otherwise fine: the lane adapter of
this router timed out three times over the same day and drained on the
first read every other time. That adapter is out of scope here, so it
would not have been flagged, but it is the shape of the thing I cannot
rule out. I settled on one because the caller already ignores the
timeout, so a false positive costs a diagnostic that was being thrown
away anyway - it is a counter away from being stricter if you would
rather it were.

I cannot show that skipping the wait is safe. Either controller can stop
answering config space entirely (probe -110, cold boot to recover); over
one day I logged 16 of those, five on one host and eleven on the other,
on the distribution kernel and on the unmodified base as much as with
anything of mine. None of them fell inside the four 200-cycle runs, which
between them are about five and a half hours of nothing but bringing the
link up and down - they cluster around module loads and deployments
instead. So the runs above cannot speak to that rate either way, and I
would rather say so than let them suggest the question is settled.

The spec question I raised in v1 is settled, and not in the direction I
had left room for. I have been through the USB4 v2 specification since,
and table 8-23 is quoted above: Pending Packets is RO and is not vendor
defined for a protocol adapter, so the driver is right to poll it and the
reading under which this patch was working around a symptom of the
connection manager's own doing is gone. What is left is one router that
never satisfies a poll the spec requires of it.

For completeness on the teardown sequence itself: the CM guide's 5.2.2
puts one step before the per-hop loop - disable sending Transport Layer
Packets on the path at the source and destination adapters. For PCIe and
USB3 that is ADP_PCIE_CS_0.Path Enable and ADP_USB3_GX_CS_0.Path Enable.
A host interface adapter has no such bit; the equivalent is quiescing the
descriptor rings, which is the caller's business rather than this
function's. I mention it because it is the one part of the sequence that
is not visible in path.c, so it is worth saying I looked at it and it is
not where this goes wrong: the pending bit already reads 1b before the
teardown starts, on an idle path with the rings quiet, so no ordering of
the steps below it can be the cause.

It would still be worth ASMedia confirming the behaviour against the NVM
above.

The other thing I would be glad of is coverage. Everything above was
measured on one model of router, and the flag is only ever set by an
adapter that has already failed to clear the bit within the existing
500 ms, so hardware that behaves should never reach it - but that is an
argument, not a measurement. If anyone with an Intel or Barlow Ridge host
can run a few hundred link cycles with this applied and confirm the flag
stays clear, I would be much happier about it than I am now.

v2:
- Add the Assisted-by tag Mika asked for.
- Name the baseline in the commit message instead of only down here.
- Answer the v1 spec question from the specification rather than leaving
  it open: quote table 8-23, which makes Pending Packets RO and not
  vendor defined for a protocol adapter.  Dropped the v1 request for
  someone else to check that field.
- State the cost of skipping the wait in the spec's own terms (5.4.1),
  instead of the vaguer "I cannot show this is safe" of v1.
- Drop the claim that the ring wrapping is what latches the bit.  The two
  ring sizes measured show the threshold moving with the ring, not a
  wrap: 120 frames is already enough on a 128 ring.
- No code change from v1.

v1: https://lore.kernel.org/linux-usb/20260809-b4-tb-teardown-v1-0-c88bbfe5c127@gmail.com/
---
 drivers/thunderbolt/path.c | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 drivers/thunderbolt/tb.h   |  3 +++
 2 files changed, 20 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/thunderbolt/path.c b/drivers/thunderbolt/path.c
index b2c322e76b8a..0dcc3deb6f61 100644
--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/path.c
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/path.c
@@ -397,6 +397,14 @@ static int __tb_path_deactivate_hop(struct tb_port *port, int hop_index,
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;

+	/*
+	 * This adapter has already been seen to leave the pending bit
+	 * set for good. The answer will not change, so give the caller
+	 * the same answer it would have got, without the wait.
+	 */
+	if (port->drain_known_stuck)
+		return -ETIMEDOUT;
+
 	/* Wait until it is drained */
 	timeout = ktime_add_ms(ktime_get(), 500);
 	do {
@@ -430,6 +438,15 @@ static int __tb_path_deactivate_hop(struct tb_port *port, int hop_index,
 		usleep_range(10, 20);
 	} while (ktime_before(ktime_get(), timeout));

+	/*
+	 * Some host interface adapters latch the pending bit and never
+	 * clear it again, and there is nothing the connection manager
+	 * can do about that from here. Remember it so the next teardown
+	 * can return this same answer without spending the timeout.
+	 */
+	if (tb_port_is_nhi(port))
+		port->drain_known_stuck = true;
+
 	return -ETIMEDOUT;
 }

diff --git a/drivers/thunderbolt/tb.h b/drivers/thunderbolt/tb.h
index ec9192b61bc0..8c022d2724e9 100644
--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/tb.h
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/tb.h
@@ -273,6 +273,8 @@ struct tb_bandwidth_group {
  * @max_bw: Maximum possible bandwidth through this adapter if set to
  *	    non-zero.
  * @redrive: For DP IN, if true the adapter is in redrive mode.
+ * @drain_known_stuck: The pending bit of this adapter's path config space
+ *		       stayed set once, so it is not waited on any more
  *
  * In USB4 terminology this structure represents an adapter (protocol or
  * lane adapter).
@@ -302,6 +304,7 @@ struct tb_port {
 	struct list_head group_list;
 	unsigned int max_bw;
 	bool redrive;
+	bool drain_known_stuck;
 };

 /**
